# Security and performance

Apply these from the mock phase so nothing needs retrofitting.

## Input, output, secrets

- Validate every input server-side (route handlers, server actions, proxy), including query params.
- Rich content (news body, newsletter content, messages, event descriptions) is sanitized before rendering. No
  `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` without the shared sanitizer.
- Only `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables may enter the client bundle. No backend keys or tokens in client code, logs or
  comments — mocks included.
- File uploads: validate type by content where possible, not only by extension; never trust client MIME.
- Exports and downloads (newsletter PDFs, reports) obey the same permission rules as the screen.

## Headers, CSRF, rate limits

- CSP, `X-Frame-Options`, `Referrer-Policy`, `Permissions-Policy` configured centrally (`next.config` / middleware).
- CSRF protection on every cookie-authenticated mutation.
- Rate limits (backend authoritative, UI respects 429): login/OTP, message send, report abuse, suggestion submit,
  referral link creation (SRS: max 3 per calendar day).

## Payments and wallet

- Event registration via wallet or payment gateway, voucher/receipt purchase: amounts from the server only,
  idempotency key on every payment mutation, no optimistic updates, gateway return handled server-side.

## Performance

- Server Components by default; `'use client'` only for interaction/state.
- Public pages (service home, news, newsletters, events, products) are server-rendered with an explicit caching /
  revalidation choice documented in `docs/04-routing.md`.
- `next/image` for all images with explicit sizes; `next/dynamic` for charts, editors, modals, file import.
- Stable `key`s in lists; no new objects/functions created inside large loops in JSX.
- No premature memoization — only after profiling shows a real problem.
